%S A400945 1,1,1,1,4,1,1,3,3,1,1,8,36,8,1,1,5,144,144,5,1,1,12,72,288,72,12,1,1,
%T A400945 7,2160,320,320,2160,7,1,1,16,840,17280,2400,17280,840,16,1,1,9,6720,
%U A400945 15120,86400,86400,15120,6720,9,1,1,20,15120,241920,1920,259200,1920,241920,15120,20,1
%N A400945 Symmetric array read by ascending antidiagonals: A(n,m) = denominator(B(n,m)), where B is defined as B(n,1)=1, B(1,m)=1 and B(n,m) = 1 + (1/(n*m))*Sum_{j=1..n-1} Sum_{k=1..m-1} B(j,k), with m > 0.
%C A400945 B(n,m) is the expected number of subtractions in the algorithm starting from an ordered pair of positive integers (n,m), where, while n > 0 and m > 0, at each next step a pair (k_1,k_2) is randomly chosen such that k_1 in [n] and k_2 in [m], and the pair (n,m) is replaced with (n-k_1,m-k_2). See Erickson reference and link.
%C A400945 Beware that the Erickson book on page 132 contains a typo for n = m = 5: 4309/2300 instead of 4309/2400.
%D A400945 Martin Erickson, Beautiful Mathematics, Mathematical Association of America, 2011, pp. 131-132.
%H A400945 Stefano Spezia, <a href="/A400945/b400945.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..11325</a> (first 150 antidiagonals, flattened)
%H A400945 Martin Erickson, <a href="https://mathoverflow.net/questions/29604/two-dimensional-gobbling-algorithm">Two-Dimensional Gobbling Algorithm</a>
%e A400945 The array of fractions begins as:
%e A400945   1,    1,       1,       1,         1, ...
%e A400945   1,  5/4,     4/3,    11/8,       7/5, ...
%e A400945   1,  4/3,   53/36, 223/144,    115/72, ...
%e A400945   1, 11/8, 223/144, 475/288,   549/320, ...
%e A400945   1,  7/5,  115/72, 549/320, 4309/2400, ...
%e A400945   ...
%t A400945 B[n_,1]:=1; B[1,m_]:=1; B[n_,m_]:=B[n,m]=1+Sum[Sum[B[j,k],{k,m-1}],{j,n-1}]/(n*m); A[n_,m_]:=Denominator[B[n,m]]; Table[A[n-m+1,m],{n,11},{m,n}]//Flatten
%Y A400945 1st column/row gives A000012.
%Y A400945 2nd column/row gives A022998.
%Y A400945 Cf. A400944 (numerators).
%K A400945 nonn,frac,tabl,new
%O A400945 1,5
